<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Sonbai</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1906 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Atoni kingdom of West Timor whose rulers held a ritual supremacy over the interior and were courted in turn by the Portuguese and the Dutch.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sonbai grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atoni">Atoni</a> (Meto) chiefdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-timor">West Timor</a> highlands, where its lords were recognised as senior ritual rulers over neighbouring domains such as Mollo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanuban">Amanuban</a>. By the 17th century it was drawn into the rivalry between the Portuguese-allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topasses">Topasses</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lifau">Lifau</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kupang">Kupang</a>, and in the 1650s it split in two.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A branch known as Small Sonbai moved near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kupang">Kupang</a> and became a Company ally, while Great Sonbai remained in the mountains. Power rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a>, alliances of lineages and tribute in kind, and the realm supplied sandalwood and beeswax to foreign traders. Most people followed the indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atoni">Atoni</a> religion centred on ancestors and the earth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Small Sonbai was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> administration around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kupang">Kupang</a>, and Great Sonbai kept its independence in the interior until the Dutch campaign of 1906 captured its last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobe-sonbai-iii">Sobe Sonbai III</a>, who died in exile. The name survives as a symbol of Timorese resistance.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1600 CE – 1906 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Maritime Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.54%;width:6.09%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1906 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sobe Sonbai III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Atoni traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Uab Meto (Dawan)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sonbai, Sonba-i</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1753&amp;focus=kingdom-of-sonbai" aria-label="Kingdom of Sonbai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7507 2226 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Sonbai lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7959 2663h0M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7827 2482h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7827 2482h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Indonesia. <a href="/atlas?year=1753&amp;focus=kingdom-of-sonbai">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
